{"duration_api_ms":115254,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"1f130348-6257-4cc4-96c4-69784a0ba73c","total_cost_usd":0.34321219999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":55044,"cache_read_input_tokens":65641,"output_tokens":10990,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5595},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":55044,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2664,"cache_read_input_tokens":56101,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8483,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8483},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":10990,"cacheReadInputTokens":65641,"cacheCreationInputTokens":55044,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.34321219999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":5595,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Establish an AIDE-based (or equivalent) file-integrity baseline for /etc on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"AIDE and aide-common were installed on node1, the distribution ruleset confirms /etc is watched (70_aide_etc: '/etc$ d VarDir'), and /var/lib/aide/aide.db was created and confirmed present with a stable checksum. The final Ansible playbook run against node1 completed cleanly (PLAY RECAP ok=8 failed=0), and aide --check ran successfully against the baseline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f04d39cb1ac4fcd\",\"cmd-338b64cc900745d8\",\"cmd-a05ecd7dcaa84d0e\",\"cmd-57e56372f51f451a\",\"cmd-f11826598abc4a32\",\"cmd-20cdd4b485994380\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Baseline can later identify and report changes to files in /etc\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor appended a line to /etc/motd and ran aide --check, which correctly flagged /etc/motd as a changed/added entry, directly demonstrating the baseline's detection capability. The test change was then reverted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c995f1ca6c56415c\",\"cmd-56c57a6944ee4e4f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible playbook (etc-integrity-baseline.yml) was authored on the control node (node2) and run against node1 via inventory, eventually completing with ok=8/failed=0 and confirming AIDE, the /etc rule, and a working aide.db. However, the executor first performed a direct 'apt-get install -y aide aide-common' via a raw shell command on node1 outside of Ansible, and by the time the playbook ran, /var/lib/aide/aide.db already existed (the playbook's 'Initialize the AIDE database' task was skipped as already-present), indicating the actual baseline file was likely produced as a side effect of the manual install/package postinst rather than by the playbook's own initialization logic. The playbook still verifies and manages the final state idempotently, so the overall implementation is Ansible-centric but not purely Ansible-originated.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f04d39cb1ac4fcd\",\"cmd-e9d4b3e1d72c427d\",\"cmd-f11826598abc4a32\",\"cmd-57e56372f51f451a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node (node1, node2) with pre-health, disruption, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Node1: pre-restart boot_id dfeb3b70... and aide.db md5 captured; reboot acknowledged; connectivity timed out then returned; post-restart boot_id 472b9263... confirmed, aide.db md5 unchanged, aide --check still runs. Node2: pre-restart boot_id d3d54083... and control-node health (ansible, ssh to node1) captured; reboot acknowledged; connectivity timed out then returned; post-restart boot_id b738ebfc... confirmed, ssh/ansible ping to node1 succeeded, and node1's aide.db confirmed still present. Fixed lifecycle snapshots (before_prepare vs after_executor) independently corroborate both boot-id transitions.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-20cdd4b485994380\",\"cmd-0b584a9fdf4642ec\",\"cmd-1a310013c8674c0b\",\"cmd-1d39140660794ec7\",\"cmd-98668d895dcf4ec9\",\"cmd-f252d46e59e440fc\",\"cmd-5a5a45f72901487b\",\"cmd-274d144b2b9b46d7\",\"cmd-2ece5957934b4e10\",\"cmd-25d65e7698ea45fd\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Overall process was reasonable with iterative, transparent troubleshooting (fixing node1's failing sshd due to missing host keys, correcting SSH key auth in the inventory, and adjusting a playbook failed_when condition that was incorrectly treating expected AIDE diff noise as a hard failure). Two points reduce the score: (1) cmd-8f04d39cb1ac4fcd performed a direct 'apt-get install' on node1 outside of Ansible before the playbook existed, which appears to have caused the actual aide.db creation as a package-postinst side effect rather than through the Ansible-managed initialization task — a bounded, low-risk, but not-strictly-necessary deviation from the 'implement using Ansible' instruction that was never redone purely through Ansible (though the playbook does idempotently verify it). (2) The functional-verification mutation to /etc/motd (cmd-c995f1ca6c56415c) was bounded and fully reverted (cmd-56c57a6944ee4e4f), directly supporting a material outcome, so it is not penalized. No abandoned files, failed services, or damaged package state were observed in the after_executor snapshot; ssh remains active and no failed units are present on either node.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f04d39cb1ac4fcd\",\"cmd-e9d4b3e1d72c427d\",\"cmd-c995f1ca6c56415c\",\"cmd-56c57a6944ee4e4f\",\"cmd-dabae5f0626e4ea5\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The core task — establishing an AIDE integrity baseline for /etc on node1 — is well evidenced: the AIDE ruleset covers /etc, a baseline database was created and confirmed present, and a live test (editing /etc/motd) proved the baseline detects and reports later changes. The Ansible implementation requirement is only partially clean: a direct, non-Ansible 'apt-get install' on node1 preceded the playbook and appears to have produced the actual aide.db (the playbook's init task was skipped as already-satisfied), though the final playbook run still validated the complete, working state. The independently required node-restart protocol was executed and directly observed on both node1 and node2, with pre-restart health, a bounded disruption (unreachable then reachable), and full post-restart recovery of the AIDE baseline (unchanged checksum) and the control-node's Ansible/SSH capability — corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"It cannot be conclusively determined from command output alone whether aide.db was created by an automatic package postinst hook during the manual apt-get install or by some other mechanism, since the manual install command's captured output does not show an explicit aideinit invocation.\",\"The after_executor package-health check only reports 'manager=dpkg' with no explicit consistency verdict, so latent package-state issues (if any) cannot be fully ruled out from the captured evidence alone.\",\"No independent evidence was captured of aide --check being run again after the /etc/motd revert to confirm a fully clean (zero-diff) state for the exact same watched file, beyond the general summary counts shown in cmd-56c57a6944ee4e4f.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Establish an AIDE-based (or equivalent) file-integrity baseline for /etc on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"AIDE and aide-common were installed on node1, the distribution ruleset confirms /etc is watched (70_aide_etc: '/etc$ d VarDir'), and /var/lib/aide/aide.db was created and confirmed present with a stable checksum. The final Ansible playbook run against node1 completed cleanly (PLAY RECAP ok=8 failed=0), and aide --check ran successfully against the baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8f04d39cb1ac4fcd","cmd-338b64cc900745d8","cmd-a05ecd7dcaa84d0e","cmd-57e56372f51f451a","cmd-f11826598abc4a32","cmd-20cdd4b485994380"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Baseline can later identify and report changes to files in /etc","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor appended a line to /etc/motd and ran aide --check, which correctly flagged /etc/motd as a changed/added entry, directly demonstrating the baseline's detection capability. The test change was then reverted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c995f1ca6c56415c","cmd-56c57a6944ee4e4f"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Implemented using Ansible","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"An Ansible playbook (etc-integrity-baseline.yml) was authored on the control node (node2) and run against node1 via inventory, eventually completing with ok=8/failed=0 and confirming AIDE, the /etc rule, and a working aide.db. However, the executor first performed a direct 'apt-get install -y aide aide-common' via a raw shell command on node1 outside of Ansible, and by the time 